 ## Overview
-Screen-Space God Rays is a real-time post-processing technique that approximates volumetric light scattering (crepuscular rays) by performing per-pixel additive sampling of an image light source in screen space [S3, book]. It simplifies the volume rendering equation to estimate single in-scattering from a single light source, modulated by an estimated occlusion factor derived from radial sampling [S5, unofficial]. Unlike slice-based volume rendering or shadow-map methods, it requires no preprocessing, extra scene geometry, or high fill rate, making it significantly cheaper than volumetric ray marching [S3, book][S4, unofficial]. However, it is limited to single light sources and lacks full volumetric depth information, leading to artifacts when foreground objects obscure background light shafts [S3, book][S4, unofficial].
+Screen-Space God Rays is a real-time post-processing technique that approximates volumetric light scattering (crepuscular rays) by performing per-pixel additive sampling of an image light source in screen space [S3, book]. It builds on [Volumetric Light Shafts (URP Raymarching)](3873-volumetric-light-shafts-urp-raymarching.md) by simplifying the volume rendering equation to estimate single in-scattering from a single light source, modulated by an estimated occlusion factor derived from radial sampling [S5, unofficial]. Unlike slice-based volume rendering or shadow-map methods, it requires no preprocessing, extra scene geometry, or high fill rate, making it significantly cheaper than volumetric ray marching [S3, book][S4, unofficial]. However, it is limited to single light sources and lacks full volumetric depth information, leading to artifacts when foreground objects obscure background light shafts [S3, book][S4, unofficial].
 
